**`!`**, the **never type**, is the type of expressions that never produce a value: `panic!()`, `continue`, `break`, `return`, `loop {}` without `break`, and `std::process::exit`. A function returning `!` is a **diverging function**.

`!` can be coerced into **any** type. That is why match arms with different "types" work:

```rust
let guess: u32 = match guess.trim().parse() {
    Ok(num) => num,
    Err(_) => continue,   // type !, fits where u32 is expected
};
```

`Option::unwrap` relies on the same rule: its `None` arm calls `panic!`, which has type `!`, so the whole match has type `T`.

